\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The hybrid advantage survives time evolution (decaying cellular flow, $256^2$, plain truncation). Top left: the $L_2$ error of the evolved field against the fraction of parameters $\rho $. Top centre: the error of merely compressing the exact field at the same budget. Top right: their ratio, the penalty for evolving. Bottom right: the error saturates in time instead of drifting. The same picture holds from $256^2$ to $2048^2$, with optimal head sizes $N\simeq L$.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hvs}{{2}{3}{The hybrid advantage survives time evolution (decaying cellular flow, $256^2$, plain truncation). Top left: the $L_2$ error of the evolved field against the fraction of parameters $\rho $. Top centre: the error of merely compressing the exact field at the same budget. Top right: their ratio, the penalty for evolving. Bottom right: the error saturates in time instead of drifting. The same picture holds from $256^2$ to $2048^2$, with optimal head sizes $N\simeq L$}{figure.2}{}}
